1
00:00:01,562 --> 00:00:10,896
This project asks a practical question: can an explainable model help people notice potentially urgent email without pretending that software should make the final decision?

2
00:00:11,188 --> 00:00:18,688
I will show the evidence gathered, what that evidence establishes today, where reliability breaks down, and what should happen next.

3
00:00:19,938 --> 00:00:22,521
The operational problem is straightforward.

4
00:00:22,813 --> 00:00:28,479
Important messages can be buried in a high-volume inbox, but urgency is not just a keyword.

5
00:00:28,771 --> 00:00:33,854
It can depend on deadlines, requests, relationships, and context.

6
00:00:34,146 --> 00:00:40,396
A useful triage tool should surface messages for attention and show why the model produced its score.

7
00:00:40,688 --> 00:00:43,438
The boundary matters just as much as the prediction.

8
00:00:43,729 --> 00:00:48,688
False negatives can hide urgent work, while false positives can overwhelm the reviewer.

9
00:00:48,979 --> 00:00:53,229
For that reason, this project treats the model as decision support.

10
00:00:53,521 --> 00:00:59,563
The system scores the message and provides an explanation, but a person remains responsible for the routing decision.

11
00:01:00,813 --> 00:01:04,396
The planned Week 8 demonstration will make that boundary visible.

12
00:01:04,688 --> 00:01:15,521
A reviewer will enter a synthetic or privacy-screened message and receive an urgency score, classifications at the two frozen thresholds, and a short list of features that supported or opposed the score.

13
00:01:15,812 --> 00:01:23,813
Those contributions explain the fitted linear calculation; they are not causal explanations and they do not prove that a message is urgent.

14
00:01:24,104 --> 00:01:32,771
The same result will state that human review is required, the disposition is revise, and neither shadow testing nor production deployment is approved.

15
00:01:33,063 --> 00:01:39,188
The application is designed not to echo the submitted subject or body into its saved validation results.

16
00:01:39,479 --> 00:01:45,271
In practical terms, the interface will expose evidence for review without automating the decision.

17
00:01:46,521 --> 00:01:53,688
The evidence begins with the Enron email corpus, which contains five hundred seventeen thousand, four hundred one source records.

18
00:01:53,979 --> 00:02:03,188
The capstone evaluation uses a much smaller reviewed sample of one hundred ninety-nine messages: thirty urgent and one hundred sixty-nine nonurgent.

19
00:02:03,479 --> 00:02:09,438
One hundred fifty-nine messages form the fixed training partition, and forty form a preliminary holdout.

20
00:02:09,729 --> 00:02:16,688
That sample supports a controlled academic evaluation, not broad claims about every inbox or production prevalence.

21
00:02:16,979 --> 00:02:28,062
The raw corpus and detailed review material stay outside the repository, while privacy-screened labels, metrics, notebooks, and decision records provide an auditable evidence trail.

22
00:02:29,312 --> 00:02:32,521
The project advanced through controlled checkpoints.

23
00:02:32,813 --> 00:02:38,896
First, the baseline established a reproducible T F I D F and logistic-regression reference.

24
00:02:39,188 --> 00:02:44,146
Next, model selection and explanation work used only the training partition.

25
00:02:44,438 --> 00:02:52,229
For Week 6, nested cross-validation separated selection from evaluation inside those one hundred fifty-nine training messages.

26
00:02:52,521 --> 00:03:00,771
Each outer fold acted as a temporary evaluation set, while the inner folds selected the threshold without seeing that outer fold's labels.

27
00:03:01,062 --> 00:03:05,313
The outer results were then combined for error analysis and the model decision.

28
00:03:05,604 --> 00:03:13,521
The preliminary holdout was examined only after the training-data decision was frozen, but earlier project work had already influenced it.

29
00:03:13,813 --> 00:03:18,021
It is therefore descriptive evidence, not a new untouched final test.

30
00:03:19,271 --> 00:03:23,188
Optimization improved the training evidence, but the gains were modest.

31
00:03:23,479 --> 00:03:38,604
Mean cross-validated urgent F one increased from six point seven percent to eleven point four percent, urgent recall doubled from four percent to eight percent, and the selected T F I D F feature count decreased by twenty-nine percent.

32
00:03:38,896 --> 00:03:42,854
These results showed that the workflow could tune and reproduce a candidate.

33
00:03:43,146 --> 00:03:46,063
They did not establish a reliable classifier.

34
00:03:46,354 --> 00:03:56,104
At the frozen zero point four five threshold, the preliminary holdout identified all six urgent messages but also labeled all thirty-four nonurgent messages urgent.

35
00:03:56,396 --> 00:04:03,646
At zero point five zero, the same holdout found two of six urgent messages, missed four, and created one false positive.

36
00:04:03,938 --> 00:04:11,146
Together, those results reinforced the need to examine threshold behavior rather than treating recall alone as success.

37
00:04:12,396 --> 00:04:16,062
Threshold testing exposed the central reliability problem.

38
00:04:16,354 --> 00:04:24,188
At zero point four five, urgent recall reached one hundred percent, but every one of the one hundred fifty-nine training messages was labeled urgent.

39
00:04:24,479 --> 00:04:30,146
That created one hundred thirty-five false positives and removed the value of prioritization.

40
00:04:30,437 --> 00:04:36,229
At zero point five zero, there were no false positives, but urgent recall fell to eight point three percent.

41
00:04:36,521 --> 00:04:40,896
The model found only two of twenty-four urgent messages and missed twenty-two.

42
00:04:41,187 --> 00:04:46,521
Another warning is that one hundred fifty-seven probabilities fell between the two thresholds.

43
00:04:46,812 --> 00:04:53,896
A change of only zero point zero five therefore moved the system from almost no urgent predictions to all urgent predictions.

44
00:04:54,187 --> 00:04:57,437
Neither threshold provides a defensible operating point.

45
00:04:58,687 --> 00:05:04,062
Week 7 software validation was evaluated separately from predictive reliability.

46
00:05:04,354 --> 00:05:07,062
All thirteen automated tests passed.

47
00:05:07,354 --> 00:05:22,812
They covered the visible research-use disclosure, valid and invalid inputs, safe behavior when the model is unavailable, notebook execution, metric traceability, privacy-sensitive columns, and preservation of the revise decision.

48
00:05:23,104 --> 00:05:31,396
A separate validator ran three synthetic cases twenty times each, for sixty total inferences, and produced identical scores within each case.

49
00:05:31,687 --> 00:05:41,479
Five Gate A checks passed: artifact boundary, deterministic scoring, non-retention in the result record, threshold visibility, and bounded explanations.

50
00:05:41,771 --> 00:05:47,312
This evidence establishes software and interface readiness for the planned Week 8 demonstration.

51
00:05:47,604 --> 00:05:50,604
It does not substitute for labeled predictive testing.

52
00:05:51,854 --> 00:05:54,229
The final disposition is revise.

53
00:05:54,521 --> 00:06:00,104
Gate A passed as an engineering-readiness check for the planned Week 8 demonstration.

54
00:06:00,396 --> 00:06:10,729
Gate B failed because neither tested threshold met the combined recall, precision, F one, review-burden, stability, calibration, and untouched-evaluation requirements.

55
00:06:11,021 --> 00:06:16,396
Human review remains required, and shadow testing and production deployment remain unapproved.

56
00:06:16,687 --> 00:06:19,771
This conclusion is not a failed project outcome.

57
00:06:20,062 --> 00:06:24,021
It is the reliability finding produced by the testing process.

58
00:06:24,312 --> 00:06:31,146
The work demonstrates a transparent pipeline, exposes the failure mode, and prevents a weak operating point from being overstated.

59
00:06:32,396 --> 00:06:38,896
My recommendation is to demonstrate the validated research workflow in Week 8 while keeping the model decision unchanged.

60
00:06:39,187 --> 00:06:52,521
The next predictive experiment should add reviewed examples, reserve a genuinely untouched test set, use related-message grouping where needed, assess calibration, and review false negatives and a reproducible sample of false positives.

61
00:06:52,812 --> 00:06:58,354
The same privacy, reproducibility, human-review, and approval controls should remain in place.

62
00:06:58,646 --> 00:07:06,687
Only a separately versioned candidate that passes the predictive gates should be considered for a bounded, supervised shadow-test recommendation.

63
00:07:06,979 --> 00:07:13,271
Even that step would require an explicit human approval record; it would not create automatic production authorization.

64
00:07:14,521 --> 00:07:25,729
The repository contains the code, executed notebooks, figures, metrics, experiment records, automated tests, validation evidence, and the Week 8 demonstration runbook.

65
00:07:26,021 --> 00:07:28,896
It is available at the GitHub address shown here.

66
00:07:29,187 --> 00:07:33,812
Questions about the project can be sent to my University of the Cumberlands email address.

67
00:07:34,104 --> 00:07:34,885
Thank you.
